Z||000000||Andy||11-20-1999||05:10 PM||||I have an oil leak what seems to be around the lower intake manifolt on the drivers side.Question: how difficult is it to change the gasket your self and does the engine has to be removed.Should I consider just having the hole engine rebuilt.I would love to put in a v8,but I'm very short on cash.I also noticed a strong fuel smell from the exhaust.<BR>The other engine problems I have noticed are: <BR>hard to start on cold,wont start when hot,oil pressure drops to around 20 when engine is hot and is ideling.Any info would greatly be appreciated.Andy||||reg||8
Z||000001||Andy||11-20-1999||05:12 PM||||forgot its an 87 gt with a v6 ||||reg||5
Z||000002||mwbackus||11-20-1999||05:29 PM||||Andy:<BR>An oil leak in the area you describe could very likely be the distributor O-ring.  Check it first, its very cheap to fix.<BR>A good diagnosis of your engine performance problems would be better allowed if a check of the ECM trouble codes were performed. Many things can cause these types of malfunction, including the "common" vacuum leak due to a cracked EGR tube.<P><BR>Mike<BR>(Fiero info in my user profile)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Linx||11-21-1999||01:05 AM||||Does the engine have to be removed?  Nah- not at all.  It's not difficult, really.  Just <I>time consuming</I>.  You've got to take off alot of stuff to get to the lower manifold gaskets; upper manifold, vacuum hoses, injector rail, intermediate manifold, valve covers...and gaskets all the way.  Not to mention the stuff that is in the way of the stuff you gotta take off...coolant hoses, wiring,  sensors, throttle body.<BR>Heck- while you're there, just go ahead and lift the heads and replace your rings, too.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||WisconsinGT||11-21-1999||10:18 AM||||Links, can you really get at the piston rings and replace them without taking the motor out? The reason I ask, is that I have a motor that is burning oil. And I am thinking that I will need to replace or rebuild my motor. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000005||mwbackus||11-21-1999||05:03 PM||||Why not just drop the cradle to rebuild an engine???  I mean sheesh, it really isnt that difficult guys.  Besides, intake manifolds rarely leak oil, they leak vacuum!  Distributor O-rings are THE most common cause of oil leaks in this area on the 2.8 V6. This only requires removal of the distributor, so why would Andy want to tear his engine half apart?<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000006||Andy||11-21-1999||05:54 PM||||Thanks guys. I will try to check my distbutor o-ring tomorrow.Sure hope that it is the o- ring,would save me a lot of work.||||reg||10
